//       rendered: '<p class="p2">Type a mood, a genre and a handful of keywords into an AI music generator, and within seconds you have a fully arranged track: vocals, instrumentation, mix, all of it. The technical achievement is undeniable. What is far less settled is what this means for the idea of a song as something a person made, meant, and is accountable for.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Composition without a composer</h5>\n<p class="p2">Traditionally, a melody carried the fingerprint of a specific decision-making process: a phrase tried, rejected, revised, played on an instrument until it felt right to a particular person at a particular moment. An AI model does something structurally different. It predicts which combination of notes and sounds statistically resembles what a listener is likely to respond to, based on patterns learned from an enormous catalogue of existing music. The output can sound convincing, even moving, without anyone having made a single deliberate choice inside it.</p>\n<p class="p2">This does not necessarily make the result worthless, but it does change what kind of object it is. A generated track is closer to a highly sophisticated average of what has worked before than to a new idea entering the world.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Where listeners draw the line</h5>\n<p class="p2">Surveys of listener attitudes consistently find a split reaction: people often cannot reliably tell AI-generated tracks apart from human-made ones in blind tests, yet they report lower emotional engagement once they learn a track was AI-generated. The sound is the same; the meaning attached to it is not. Knowing that a specific person struggled, failed and eventually got it right seems to be part of what listeners are responding to, independent of the acoustic result itself.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">A tool, or a replacement?</h5>\n<p class="p2">Many working musicians already use AI as an instrument: to sketch a chord progression, generate a rough drum pattern, or explore a direction quickly before committing studio time to it. Used this way, the technology functions the way a sampler or a drum machine once did, expanding what a single person can attempt. The tension arises when the tool is not assisting a human decision but replacing the need for one entirely, from prompt to finished, released product.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Authorship as a moving target</h5>\n<p class="p2">Music history already contains precedents for redefining what counts as authorship, from sampling to collaborative production to ghostwriting. AI composition is a more radical version of the same question, not an entirely new one. What seems likely is not that machine-generated music disappears, but that listeners, platforms and eventually regulation will start distinguishing more explicitly between what a person made and what a system produced on their behalf, and that this distinction will matter more, not less, as the technology gets better at hiding the difference.</p>'
