//     content: {
//       rendered: '<p class="p2">Every year, a wave of beginners gets talked into spending far more than necessary on their first setup, on the assumption that better gear automatically means better recordings. In practice, the biggest jump in quality rarely comes from the equipment list. It comes from three unglamorous decisions made early and made correctly.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">The room matters more than the microphone</h5>\n<p class="p2">A mediocre microphone in a well-treated room will consistently outperform an expensive microphone in an untreated one. Reflections off bare walls, parallel surfaces and hard floors are what make home recordings sound distinctly amateur, not the price of the capsule. Basic acoustic treatment, even a few strategically placed panels and a rug, closes more of the gap between home and professional recordings than any single piece of hardware will.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">One good interface beats three mediocre plugins</h5>\n<p class="p2">An audio interface converts sound into digital data before anything else happens to it; if that conversion is poor, no amount of processing afterward will fix it. Beginners often spend their entire budget on plugins and virtual instruments while running everything through the cheapest interface available. A mid-range interface with clean preamps is a better investment than a bundle of software effects, most of which come free with the recording software anyway.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Monitoring decides whether your mixes translate</h5>\n<p class="p2">Mixing on headphones that flatter the low end, or speakers placed against a wall, produces mixes that sound wrong everywhere else. This is the single most common reason a beginner\'s track sounds fine in their own room and thin, muddy or harsh on every other system. A pair of honest, accurately reproducing monitors or headphones, positioned correctly, is not optional if the goal is a mix that holds up outside your own four walls.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">What to actually spend money on first</h5>\n<p class="p2">In order of impact for most beginners: basic room treatment, a decent interface with clean preamps, one versatile microphone rather than several specialized ones, and accurate monitoring. Everything else, including most plugins, can be added gradually as specific needs come up. Buying in that order means every dollar spent visibly improves what you can hear, rather than sitting unused in a folder of presets you will never fully explore.</p>'
