//     },
//     content: {
//       rendered: '<p class="p2">Streaming pays out from a shared pool: subscribers pay a flat fee, platforms take a cut, and what remains is distributed among rights holders based on their share of total streams that month. This structure sounds neutral, but it quietly favors the artists who already dominate listening, at the direct expense of everyone else.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">The pool model, explained</h5>\n<p class="p2">Under this system, known as pro rata, an independent artist with a small but loyal audience is competing for the same shrinking pool as the most streamed acts on the planet. A stream from a fan who plays nothing else all month and a stream from a fan with a hundred artists in rotation are worth exactly the same to the artist who receives it, but the total money in the pool does not grow proportionally with genuine engagement. It grows with subscriber numbers, largely unrelated to how devoted any individual listener is to any individual artist.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Why per-stream rates look so small</h5>\n<p class="p2">Reported per-stream payouts, often cited as fractions of a cent, are not a fixed price set by the platform; they are simply the pool divided by total streams, and they fall further once you account for the platform\'s cut, the distributor\'s cut, and the split between the artist and any label, publisher, session musicians or collaborators attached to the recording. What reaches the artist directly is frequently a small remainder of an already small number.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">The alternative that most listeners never hear about</h5>\n<p class="p2">A user-centric model would instead allocate each subscriber\'s monthly fee only to the artists that specific subscriber actually listened to, rather than pooling it with everyone else\'s. Independent research modeling this alternative has found it would shift meaningful money toward niche and emerging artists and away from the small number of acts that already receive a disproportionate share of total plays. Several platforms have tested or partially implemented versions of this model, but pro rata remains the industry default.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">What actually changes an artist\'s income</h5>\n<p class="p2">Given the structure of the pool, the artists who do well on streaming income are almost always those who supplement it: touring, merchandise, sync licensing, direct fan support platforms. Streaming functions less as a primary income source for most working musicians and more as discovery infrastructure, a way to be heard that then has to be converted into revenue somewhere else. Understanding that distinction changes how the entire royalty debate should be read: the complaint is rarely that streaming pays too little per play in isolation, but that it was never built to be anyone\'s main source of income in the first place.</p>'
