    slug: 'why-vinyl-refuses-to-die-analog-listening-in-a-streaming-age',
    title: 'Why Vinyl Refuses to Die: Analog Listening in a Streaming Age',
    description: 'Sales of vinyl records keep climbing even as streaming dominates every chart. What does it mean to choose friction over convenience?',
    cover: 'https://drummersblog.de/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Spotify-Playlists-und-die-algorithmische-Neuordnung-des-Musiksammelns-scaled-768x432.jpg',
    date: '12.01.26',
    alt: 'Why Vinyl Refuses to Die: Analog Listening in a Streaming Age',
    autor: 'Mara Voss',
    mainCategory: 'Culture',
    counters: {
      views: 872,
      likes: 34,
      facebookCounter: 6
    },
    content: {
      rendered: '<p class="p2">For more than a decade now, industry analysts have predicted that vinyl\'s comeback was a fad about to fade. It never has. Pressing plants are backlogged for months, record store shelves keep expanding, and a growing share of buyers are people who grew up entirely within the streaming era and never owned a turntable before last year.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Convenience was never the whole story</h5>\n<p class="p2">Streaming solved a real problem: instant access to nearly everything ever recorded, for a flat monthly fee. But access is not the same as ownership, and infinite choice is not the same as commitment. A vinyl record asks something of the listener that a playlist never does. You have to get up, choose one album out of the ones you own, place the needle, and sit with the sequence the artist intended, in the order they intended it.</p>\n<p class="p2">That friction is precisely what many listeners say they are seeking. In a media environment built to minimize effort, the deliberate inconvenience of a physical format becomes a feature rather than a bug.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">A different relationship with attention</h5>\n<p class="p2">Streaming platforms are optimized to keep you listening, which usually means keeping you listening passively. Vinyl does the opposite: side lengths are short, so the listener returns to the record every fifteen or twenty minutes, an interruption that streaming was explicitly designed to remove. Fans describe this as a kind of forced mindfulness, a small ritual that keeps the act of listening from dissolving into background noise.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Ownership as a statement</h5>\n<p class="p2">There is also an economic dimension. Streaming pays artists fractions of a cent per play, and a subscription can be cancelled without leaving a trace of what was ever listened to. Buying a record is a direct, visible transaction with a specific artist, and it leaves something behind on a shelf. For listeners who want their relationship with an artist to be more than a data point in a recommendation algorithm, that visibility matters.</p>\n<h5 class="p2">Not a rejection of streaming, but a complement to it</h5>\n<p class="p2">Few vinyl buyers are abandoning streaming altogether; most use it to discover new music and then buy the records that mattered enough to keep. The two formats increasingly serve different functions: streaming for breadth and discovery, vinyl for depth and permanence. Understood that way, the vinyl revival is not nostalgia for its own sake. It is listeners drawing a line between what they consume and what they choose to keep.</p>'
    },
